{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And he recognized them and made himself strange to them, etc.</b> That the verse repeated and said a second time, ״וַיַּכֵּר יוֹסֵף אֶת אֶחָיו״ (Genesis 42:8), is because the first recognition refers to the form of their faces — that is, he recognized who they were — and the second recognition refers to matters of brotherhood and compassion, as Rashi explained. And in the Akedah it was explained: at first, ״וַיַּכִּירֵם וַיִּתְנַכֵּר״ — he recognized and he did not recognize; and after they said ״מֵאֶרֶץ כְּנַעַן בָּאוּ״ (Genesis 42:7), then he recognized them with a complete recognition, that they were his brothers. And the Ibn Ezra explained that at first he was in doubt who was Reuben and who was Simeon, and afterward, when he reflected upon them, he recognized them with a complete recognition — who was who among those who walked." "Now regarding the matter of harsh speech, every thoughtful person will be astounded: what did Yosef see to distress his father and his brothers for nothing? And that which the Ramban explained, that he did all this in order that the dreams be fulfilled, etc. — if HaShem wills their fulfillment, they will be fulfilled on their own; and what did Yosef accomplish? And what appears to me in this matter is that the reason he did not yet reveal to his father that he was alive is because he reasoned: if the Holy One, blessed be He, did not reveal it to him, then the Holy One, blessed be He, desired his distress for twenty-two years, measure for measure; and how could he reveal what the Holy One, blessed be He, had covered? For he found through his reasoning that there had been decreed upon his father distress for twenty-two complete years, day for day, corresponding to those twenty-two years during which he did not fulfill the commandment of honoring his father; and after they were completed, then Yosef made himself known to his brothers. And as for his distressing his brothers — he did all this to purge their sin in having sold their brother, and their sin is too great to bear and they require the purging of sufferings, measure for measure." "And close to this did Maharia explain, and I add to his words to explain all the details and the events. For the first accusation of ״מְרַגְּלִים אַתֶּם״ — you are spies — was to purge the sin that they considered Yosef to be a spy and a talebearer, coming to see the nakedness of his brothers. For when he came to them at Dotan it is written ״וּבְטֶרֶם יִקְרַב אֲלֵיהֶם״ — and before he came near to them — and so forth (Genesis 37:18); for they thought that his desire was to draw near to them and to spy out what their brothers were doing, so as to go back and bring some evil report to their father about them. And every talebearer causes bloodshed, as it is written (Ezekiel 22:9), ״אַנְשֵׁי רָכִיל הָיוּ בָךְ לְמַעַן שְׁפָךְ דָם״ — men of slander were in you for the purpose of shedding blood. Therefore, ״בְּטֶרֶם יִקְרַב אֲלֵיהֶם״ — before he came near to them — to kill them, they plotted to kill him; for they reasoned: he who comes to kill you — anticipate him and kill him before he draws near to you. And therefore it says ״וַיִּזְכֹּר אֶת הַחֲלֹמוֹת״ (Genesis 42:9) — and he remembered the dreams — before he said to them ״מְרַגְּלִים אַתֶּם.״ For he recalled that he had dreamed ״וְהִנֵּה תְסֻבֶּינָה אֲלֻמֹּתֵיכֶם״ (Genesis 37:7) — and behold, your sheaves would circle — and it did not explain what the circling was, but surely their end would be in affairs of spies, for every spy goes and circles the entire city to see from which direction it is easy to conquer. And a proof is from the fact that they entered through ten gates of the city, going around and around. And through this circling they would bow down to Yosef's sheaf, for they would come with the pretext of ״מְרַגְּלִים אַתֶּם לִרְאוֹת אֶת עֶרְוַת הָאָרֶץ בָּאתֶם״ (Genesis 42:9). And through this the sin was purged — that they had considered Yosef a spy coming to see the nakedness of their deeds. And corresponding to the fact that they cast Yosef into the pit: ״וַיֶּאֱסֹף אוֹתָם אֶל־מִשְׁמָר״ (Genesis 42:17) — Rashi explained: a house of imprisonment, that is, a pit, as it is written of Yosef, ״וַיִּתְּנֵהוּ אֶל בֵּית הַסֹּהַר״ (Genesis 39:20), and Yosef said, ״כִּי שָׂמוּ אוֹתִי בַבּוֹר״ (Genesis 41:14). And Rashi was compelled to explain thus, for if the house of detention were in some room, it would not have been measure for measure. Therefore, ״וַיִּקַּח מֵאִתָּם אֶת שִׁמְעוֹן״ (Genesis 42:24) — for he was the one who cast him into the pit." "And they said by way of confession: ״אֲבָל אֲשֵׁמִים אֲנַחְנוּ״ — but we are guilty — and so forth (Genesis 42:21), because they heard that Yosef said ״אֶת הָאֱלֹהִים אֲנִי יָרֵא״ — I fear Elohim — and so forth (Genesis 42:18), and they saw the truth that it was so, for he had acted kindly toward them and sent provisions for their households and detained none but Simeon. If so, one cannot hang all these events on a harsh and cruel ruler — for behold, he fears Elohim — but surely their own sins had caused these things. Therefore they confessed after he said ״אֶת הָאֱלֹהִים אֲנִי יָרֵא,״ and not before that, when he put all of them in prison — for afterward they saw eye to eye that with the very measure they had measured, it was measured back to them." "And the affair of the goblet was so that through him they would be in dread of servitude, as they pronounced upon themselves: ״הִנֶּנּוּ עֲבָדִים לַאדֹנִי״ — behold, we are servants to my lord — for through this the sin of having sold Yosef into servitude would be purged. And when they recounted all the events to Yaakov, he said ״אִם כֵּן אֵיפוֹא זֹאת עֲשׂוּ.״ What is the meaning of the word ״אֵיפוֹא״? Rather, he said: if this is the measure that comes forth, exacted against you in measure-for-measure — ״בְּסַאסְּאָה בְּשַׁלְחָהּ תְּרִיבֶנָּה״ (Isaiah 27:8) — and it is not by chance but by providence, ״זֹאת עֲשׂוּ: קְחוּ מִזִּמְרַת הָאָרֶץ בִּכְלֵיכֶם״ — this do: take of the song of the land in your vessels — and so forth, in order to atone for the fact that they sold him to Ishmaelites bearing balm and gum and myrrh. Thus they would bring a gift from these same species, so that through this parallel the entire sin would be purged. And even though Yaakov did not know from any recognition, nevertheless the spirit of HaShem spoke through him to bring a gift from these species. ״וְאֵל שַׁדַּי יִתֵּן לָכֶם רַחֲמִים״ — and El Shaddai grant you mercy — for through this would be purged the sin that they showed no mercy to their brother when he pleaded with them."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 42, :notes {0 "The reference to 'the Akedah' appears to cite the commentary known as 'Akeidat Yitzchak' by R. Isaac Arama, which the text spells here as בעקדה rather than the more common abbreviation מהרי״א used later for the same author.", 1 "In the phrase 'if HaShem wills their fulfillment,' the abbreviation ה׳ in the source has been rendered as HaShem per the rule for divine names.", 2 "Maharia (מהר״י א) in comment 2 appears to be the same figure as 'the Akedah' in comment 0; I have transliterated the abbreviation as it stands, where comment 0's reference likely points to the same work, Akeidat Yitzchak of R. Isaac Arama.", 3 "The divine name אֱלֹהִים in Joseph's statement and the brothers' reaction is rendered as Elohim per the rule for divine names.", 4 "The citation from Isaiah is given as (ישעיה כז, ח) with a comma separating chapter and verse in the Hebrew convention; I have normalized it to (Isaiah 27:8). The wordplay on אֵיפוֹא / אֵיפָה (a homophonic pun meaning 'measure') is rendered as 'measure that comes forth' to keep the paronomasia visible."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:33:26.977213058Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}